(E'PT157-S3-Q25',E'lr_explanation_choice_notes',E'body',E'fa758a01-be00-4123-be04-47a3fdc10084',E'b378e1b0-88e3-450f-9bdf-10644a048ea1',E'D',NULL,E'The placebo group was eating vitamin C-rich foods? If anything, that helps the argument. If both groups were getting vitamin C (one through pills, one through diet) and there was no difference, that is more evidence vitamin C does not help with acne. This answer accidentally supports the conclusion instead of weakening it.',E'992dbdb6768119db9ba315d2bb9621cb',E'Suppose vitamin C does help, but some placebo subjects get it from food. Their improvement could make the groups look alike even though the vitamin works. That would undermine the conclusion, not support it.\n\nD leaves too much open, though. How much vitamin C did those subjects get? Were they among the subjects who couldn''t tell which pill they had? The answer doesn''t say. A gives a clearer reason the comparison could hide a benefit: the vitamin C group had a history of worse acne.',E'707b20f79713abb4ca3f73b0cc41cc8a'),
(E'PT157-S3-Q25',E'lr_explanation_choice_notes',E'body',E'b4f8ee68-7f98-489f-9339-ff0242629f75',E'882d0369-ee56-4170-93df-3b78be87975c',E'D',NULL,E'If the placebo group also got vitamin C through food, the lack of difference between groups still suggests the pills added no benefit. This supports the conclusion rather than weakening it.',E'9c7a7cc22ea2105635e420466da59f38',E'Vitamin C in the placebo group''s food could blur the comparison, which weakens rather than supports the conclusion. But D only says some subjects ate those foods. We don''t know the dose or whether those subjects were in the blind comparison. A gives stronger evidence: the vitamin C group had a history of worse acne.',E'5a66fe497c45163f173e0d004383a039'),
(E'PT157-S3-Q25',E'lr_answer_choices',E'tag',E'75ee6f0d-1181-4f16-90d8-cb8c768d6e42',NULL,E'D',NULL,E'Strengthens',E'edaf7be68b7a514ddc7cde0bb5b1e585',E'Too Weak',E'ce0120b1ff9299fa01209aa62720c95d'),
(E'PT141-S2-Q5',E'lr_questions',E'stimulus',E'b101f714-e4a4-46fa-8642-ac3fd459ee5f',NULL,E'',NULL,E'Science writer: Lemaitre argued that the universe began with the explosion of a "primeval in atom," a singular point of infinite gravity in space and time. If this is correct, our current observations should reveal galaxies accelerating away from one another. This is precisely what we observe. Yet because there is another theory—the oscillating universe theory—that makes exactly this same prediction, Lemaitre''s theory must be considered inadequate.',E'dc75d1c6a64e8377435d1b31cefc7cd1',E'Science writer: Lemaître argued that the universe began with the explosion of a "primeval atom," a singular point of infinite gravity in space and time. If this is correct, our current observations should reveal galaxies accelerating away from one another. This is precisely what we observe. Yet because there is another theory—the oscillating universe theory—that makes exactly this same prediction, Lemaître''s theory must be considered inadequate.',E'4d53dd8f668d7fc024ad23bb240c9b0d'),
(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_questions',E'stimulus',E'a6f85102-12cf-4ec1-9cba-812d75ff4b5c',NULL,E'',NULL,E'Dr. Khan: Professor Bums recognizes that recent observations fail to confirm earlier ones that apparently showed a comet reservoir far out in our solar system. She claims this nonconfirmation is enough to show that the earlier observations are incorrect. But the recent observations occurred under poor conditions.',E'8cd4cc4d629717733206512a826c99a6',E'Dr. Khan: Professor Burns recognizes that recent observations fail to confirm earlier ones that apparently showed a comet reservoir far out in our solar system. She claims this nonconfirmation is enough to show that the earlier observations are incorrect. But the recent observations occurred under poor conditions.',E'fb5fc99584c029320d471c1ae5202d62'),
(E'PT108-S2-Q18',E'lr_questions',E'stimulus',E'44c33d89-1b57-4844-9d8e-3bec2e953a9a',NULL,E'',NULL,E'Sid: The sign says "Keep off the grass:"\n\nMicki: I know, but just one person walking across the grass doesn''t hurt it.\n\nSid: Your statement is false. If everyone believed as you do, everyone would walk across the grass, and the grass would die.',E'38e820f6ba5a48aeab040232261cc01b',E'Sid: The sign says "Keep off the grass."\n\nMicki: I know, but just one person walking across the grass doesn''t hurt it.\n\nSid: Your statement is false. If everyone believed as you do, everyone would walk across the grass, and the grass would die.',E'24a79cc967ad2d9b2e10f08c6aafcf6c'),
(E'PT143-S4-Q1',E'lr_questions',E'stimulus',E'd69be8e3-91c0-4621-a616-7eed636de408',NULL,E'',NULL,E'Among Trinidadians guppies, males with large spots are more attractive to females than are males with small spots, who consequently are presented with less frequent mating opportunities. Yet guppies with small spots are more likely to avoid detection by predators, so in waters where predators are abundant only guppies with small spots live to maturity.',E'abf17a1a824f62459d50a83b5519ea1d',E'Among Trinidadian guppies, males with large spots are more attractive to females than are males with small spots, who consequently are presented with less frequent mating opportunities. Yet guppies with small spots are more likely to avoid detection by predators, so in waters where predators are abundant only guppies with small spots live to maturity.',E'3cabe46133b878783ba42c49a5fb81d6'),
(E'PT108-S3-Q6',E'lr_explanation_steps',E'body',E'30050c0b-3c74-407f-a7ab-42bd91a5178d',E'7aca97bc-3629-4277-bbd4-5597bdda2c4c',E'2',E'Evaluate',E'This isn''t a true paradox. Nothing here is surprising. The stem just wants explanations for a drop in the number of bass caught.\r\n\r\nThink about what "number of bass caught" depends on. It takes two things: bass in the water and people fishing for them. So there are two roads to fewer bass caught:\r\n1. **Fewer bass downstream.** They died, got eaten, moved away, or lost their habitat.\r\n2. **Less fishing downstream.** Fewer anglers, or anglers fishing less.\r\n\r\nExpect most of the answers we eliminate to come in on road 1, with at least one sneaking in on road 2.\r\n\r\nMost students only think of road 1, because the wastewater feels like it must be hurting the fish. But road 2 is just as good. If half the anglers stop showing up, half as many bass get caught, even if the bass are thriving.',E'c9fe331374bbbe17c7ac3f9ed1a95db6',E'This isn''t a true paradox. Nothing here is surprising. The stem just wants explanations for a drop in the number of bass caught.\n\nThink about what "number of bass caught" depends on. It takes two things: bass in the water and people fishing for them. So there are two roads to fewer bass caught:\n1. **Fewer bass downstream.** They died, got eaten, moved away, or lost their habitat.\n2. **Less fishing downstream.** Fewer anglers, or anglers fishing less.\n\nExpect most of the answers we eliminate to come in on road 1, with at least one sneaking in on road 2.\n\nMost students only think of road 1, because the wastewater feels like it must be hurting the fish. But road 2 is just as good. If half the anglers stop showing up, half as many bass get caught, even if the bass are thriving.',E'1ccdbd3ca089eac750b68e5cf4223598'),
(E'PT108-S3-Q6',E'lr_explanation_steps',E'body',E'3a915989-8077-4179-8156-11839c6a5952',E'7aca97bc-3629-4277-bbd4-5597bdda2c4c',E'3',E'Goal',E'Eliminate four answers that explain fewer bass caught, by either road. The correct answer will do neither, or it will push the other way: more bass or more fishing, which should mean *more* bass caught.\r\n\r\nBe careful with any answer about anglers. It isn''t out of scope. Anglers are half the equation.',E'3c26722b61516a64cf1806abf64423e2',E'Eliminate four answers that explain fewer bass caught, by either road. The correct answer will do neither, or it will push the other way: more bass or more fishing, which should mean *more* bass caught.\n\nBe careful with any answer about anglers. It isn''t out of scope. Anglers are half the equation.',E'2a912b4ff39edb7a272df9230230e31a'),
