(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_answer_choices',E'choice_text',E'5382a509-006b-4ca9-a7da-4f143167bf4d',NULL,E'B',NULL,E'Contrary to Professor Bums''s view, the recent observations confirm the earlier ones.',E'48c120e13b8760413e56d8dc05e45135',E'Contrary to Professor Burns''s view, the recent observations confirm the earlier ones.',E'c4f95ca57eb599a819bb8d8a246d674f'),
(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_answer_choices',E'choice_text',E'e5dfb652-c5e4-4454-92b9-edc47efa8528',NULL,E'C',NULL,E'Professor Bums''s claim about the implications of the recent observations is incorrect.',E'b7bfe6f9bd512b122a7c3a1fb38ad023',E'Professor Burns''s claim about the implications of the recent observations is incorrect.',E'169b9b1d8a113ef8d4a3d578b3cc79ae'),
(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_explanation_steps',E'body',E'8c372d18-2721-49a8-b820-0b191e9d476a',E'577fb3da-65b1-420a-aa0f-261d60c7bf05',E'1',E'Evaluate',E'We have to distinguish between factual claims and attributed opinions sometimes on Inference questions, but the first sentence is both.\nSaying that "Professor Bums recognizes X" means both that X is true (according to the speaker, whose statements we are accepting as true) and that Professor Bums believes it.\nThere isn''t any Conditional, Causal, or Mathematical wording in this paragraph but there is a Pivot. Typically, if a Most Supported paragraph ends with a Pivot sentence, the correct answer represents a safely worded way of resolving the tension created by the pivot word. We call that sort of pattern Reconcile the Pivot.\nThe tension here is that Professor Bums thinks that new observations refute old observations, but the speaker is pointing out that these new observations occurred under poor conditions.\nThis seems to suggest that the new data points aren''t super trustworthy. Would we be a little hasty to toss out the old observations as invalid, based off some sketchy new observations?',E'f88c029f45ba3361ea5a8cb60db2c30e',E'We have to distinguish between factual claims and attributed opinions sometimes on Inference questions, but the first sentence is both.\nSaying that "Professor Burns recognizes X" means both that X is true (according to the speaker, whose statements we are accepting as true) and that Professor Burns believes it.\nThere isn''t any Conditional, Causal, or Mathematical wording in this paragraph but there is a Pivot. Typically, if a Most Supported paragraph ends with a Pivot sentence, the correct answer represents a safely worded way of resolving the tension created by the pivot word. We call that sort of pattern Reconcile the Pivot.\nThe tension here is that Professor Burns thinks that new observations refute old observations, but the speaker is pointing out that these new observations occurred under poor conditions.\nThis seems to suggest that the new data points aren''t super trustworthy. Would we be a little hasty to toss out the old observations as invalid, based off some sketchy new observations?',E'368abf5845fb4addb807b738abd09280'),
(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_explanation_choice_notes',E'body',E'e91824f1-a6b1-437a-9fda-973eeb6bcef8',E'577fb3da-65b1-420a-aa0f-261d60c7bf05',E'B',NULL,E'This flies too far in the right direction. The author is pointing out that the recent observations are sketchy to push back a little at Bums''s view that the new data refutes the old data.\n\nOur author is merely suggesting, "Whoa ... these aren''t great observations. They were under poor conditions. Let''s not draw any definitive conclusions about the old observations based on these sketchy ones."\n\nThis answer is saying, "Whoa .. these aren''t great observations. Thus, you''re dead wrong, Bums. They actually confirm the earlier observations."',E'c6e1aad8391aadfd45d8ec6269a836c3',E'This flies too far in the right direction. The author is pointing out that the recent observations are sketchy to push back a little at Burns''s view that the new data refutes the old data.\n\nOur author is merely suggesting, "Whoa ... these aren''t great observations. They were under poor conditions. Let''s not draw any definitive conclusions about the old observations based on these sketchy ones."\n\nThis answer is saying, "Whoa .. these aren''t great observations. Thus, you''re dead wrong, Burns. They actually confirm the earlier observations."',E'0da52b420efa11adc112e3958a55149c'),
(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_explanation_choice_notes',E'body',E'414818a5-50b9-4acf-84f1-41110cd57212',E'577fb3da-65b1-420a-aa0f-261d60c7bf05',E'C',NULL,E'This still sounds more definitive than I''d expect (Bums''s claim is incorrect), but this is the most supportable answer available.\n\nThe author is definitely suggesting that we''d be hasty to throw out the old observations based on these sketchy new ones. So our author disagrees that these new observations imply that the old ones are incorrect.\n\nBums''s claim was extreme, so denying it is a moderate idea.\n\nBums was like, "These new observations are enough / are sufficient ... these new observations prove that the earlier ones were wrong."\n\nBy saying Bums''s claim is incorrect, we''re only saying, "Whoa --- the new observations don''t prove the old ones were wrong".',E'addd8a3f22b62562d3df98467aef467d',E'This still sounds more definitive than I''d expect (Burns''s claim is incorrect), but this is the most supportable answer available.\n\nThe author is definitely suggesting that we''d be hasty to throw out the old observations based on these sketchy new ones. So our author disagrees that these new observations imply that the old ones are incorrect.\n\nBurns''s claim was extreme, so denying it is a moderate idea.\n\nBurns was like, "These new observations are enough / are sufficient ... these new observations prove that the earlier ones were wrong."\n\nBy saying Burns''s claim is incorrect, we''re only saying, "Whoa --- the new observations don''t prove the old ones were wrong".',E'0efd9ba8754f327568c3d9b9aa4aca24'),
(E'PT143-S1-Q13',E'lr_explanation_choice_notes',E'body',E'5dcc5f41-dcce-4ef6-8b8d-fa2be7982dac',E'577fb3da-65b1-420a-aa0f-261d60c7bf05',E'E',NULL,E'This also goes too far in the correct direction. The author is pointing out that the recent observations are sketchy, not worthless.\n\nHe''s just trying to push back a little at Bums''s view that the new data refutes the old data.\n\nOur author is merely suggesting, "Hey ... these aren''t great observations. They were under poor conditions. Let''s not draw any definitive conclusions about the old observations based on these sketchy ones."\n\nBut it''s taking it to a whole other level to say the recent observations are worthless.',E'3ed29d1472ea0ad8532a37749c5aa011',E'This also goes too far in the correct direction. The author is pointing out that the recent observations are sketchy, not worthless.\n\nThe speaker is just trying to push back a little at Burns''s view that the new data refutes the old data.\n\nOur author is merely suggesting, "Hey ... these aren''t great observations. They were under poor conditions. Let''s not draw any definitive conclusions about the old observations based on these sketchy ones."\n\nBut it''s taking it to a whole other level to say the recent observations are worthless.',E'1cb2407b9b12704ce09a1afb01a23db0')),

actual as (
 select q.qid,'lr_questions'::text tbl,'stimulus'::text col,q.id::text rid,null::text eid,''::text slot,null::text label,q.stimulus body from lr_questions q where q.qid in(select qid from expected_questions)
 union all select q.qid,'lr_answer_choices','tag',c.id::text,null,c.letter::text,null,c.tag from lr_answer_choices c join lr_questions q on q.id=c.question_id where c.id::text in(select rid from expected_edits)
 union all select q.qid,'lr_answer_choices','choice_text',c.id::text,null,c.letter::text,null,c.choice_text from lr_answer_choices c join lr_questions q on q.id=c.question_id where c.id::text in(select rid from expected_edits)
 union all select q.qid,'lr_explanation_steps','body',s.id::text,e.id::text,s.position::text,s.label,s.body from lr_explanation_steps s join lr_explanations e on e.id=s.explanation_id join lr_questions q on q.id=e.question_id where s.id::text in(select rid from expected_edits)
 union all select q.qid,'lr_explanation_choice_notes','body',n.id::text,e.id::text,n.letter::text,null,n.body from lr_explanation_choice_notes n join lr_explanations e on e.id=n.explanation_id join lr_questions q on q.id=e.question_id where n.id::text in(select rid from expected_edits)),
checked as (select e.qid,e.tbl,e.col,e.rid,
 (select count(*) from actual a where a.qid=e.qid and a.tbl=e.tbl and a.col=e.col and a.rid=e.rid and (e.eid is null or a.eid=e.eid) and a.slot=e.slot and (e.label is null or a.label=e.label) and a.body=e.new_text and md5(a.body)=e.new_md5) matches from expected_edits e),
question_checks as (select x.qid,
 (select count(*) from lr_questions t where t.id::text=x.rid and t.qid=x.qid and md5(t.stimulus)=x.stimulus_md5 and md5(t.prompt)=x.prompt_md5 and t.correct_choice::text=x.key) matches from expected_questions x),
choice_checks as (select x.qid,x.letter,
 (select count(*) from lr_answer_choices c join lr_questions q on q.id=c.question_id where q.qid=x.qid and c.id::text=x.rid and c.letter::text=x.letter and md5(c.choice_text)=x.body_md5 and c.is_correct=x.correct::boolean) matches from expected_choices x)
select jsonb_build_object('expected_rows',16,'matching_rows',(select count(*) from checked where matches=1),
 'mismatches',coalesce((select jsonb_agg(to_jsonb(c)) from checked c where matches<>1),'[]'::jsonb),
 'question_snapshot_mismatches',coalesce((select jsonb_agg(to_jsonb(c)) from question_checks c where matches<>1),'[]'::jsonb),
 'choice_snapshot_mismatches',coalesce((select jsonb_agg(to_jsonb(c)) from choice_checks c where matches<>1),'[]'::jsonb),
 'extra_choice_rows',(select count(*) from lr_answer_choices c join lr_questions q on q.id=c.question_id where q.qid in(select qid from expected_questions) and not exists(select 1 from expected_choices x where x.rid=c.id::text)),
 'standard_preserved',(select count(*) from lr_explanation_choice_notes n join lr_explanations e on e.id=n.explanation_id join lr_questions q on q.id=e.question_id where n.id::text=E'011c47e1-9daa-4d67-8a2d-7bff9623600d' and md5(n.body)=E'791a7db40fa7521728cb22c30af4ebb0' and q.qid=E'PT157-S3-Q25' and e.style=E'standard')) audit;
